              "text": "The best and most common way to change the color of a `<span>` on mouseover is using **CSS**. However, you can also do this using **JavaScript** if you need more dynamic control. \n\nHere are the three best ways to do it:\n\n---\n\n### Method 1: Using CSS (Recommended / Best Practice)\nUsing the CSS `:hover` pseudo-class is the cleanest and most performant way to handle hover states.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <style>\n        /* Target the span using a class */\n        .hover-text {\n            color: black; /* Default color */\n            cursor: pointer; /* Optional: changes cursor to hand */\n            transition: color 0.3s ease; /* Optional: makes the transition smooth */\n        }\n\n        /* Change color when mouse hovers over it */\n        .hover-text:hover {\n            color: red; /* Hover color */\n        }\n    </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n\n    <span class=\"hover-text\">Hover over me to change my color!</span>\n\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n---\n\n### Method 2: Inline JavaScript (Quickest)\nIf you cannot use an external or internal stylesheet, you can use inline JavaScript using the `onmouseover` and `onmouseout` attributes.\n\n```html\n<span \n  style=\"color: black;\" \n  onmouseover=\"this.style.color='red'\" \n  onmouseout=\"this.style.color='black'\">\n  Hover over me to change my color!\n</span>\n```\n\n---\n\n### Method 3: JavaScript Event Listeners (For Dynamic Control)\nIf you want to keep your HTML clean and handle the hover state entirely in a JavaScript file, you can use `addEventListener`.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <style>\n        #my-span {\n            color: black;\n        }\n    </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n\n    <span id=\"my-span\">Hover over me to change my color!</span>\n\n    <script>\n        const span = document.getElementById('my-span');\n\n        // Change color on mouse enter\n        span.addEventListener('mouseenter', () => {\n            span.style.color = 'red';\n        });\n\n        // Revert color on mouse leave\n        span.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => {\n            span.style.color = 'black';\n        });\n    </script>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n### Which one should you choose?\n* Use **Method 1 (CSS)** for 99% of use cases. It is faster, better for performance, and keeps your styling separate from your logic.\n* Use **Method 2 or 3 (JavaScript)** only if the color needs to change dynamically based on programming logic (e.g., pulling a random color from a database or a user setting).",
